This code uses the libgpiod library to control the GPIO pins on the Raspberry Pi 3 Model B+. It sets up four GPIO lines as output, initially setting them to LOW (inactive). It then enters a loop where it activates each relay in sequence for 1 second, then deactivates it, with a 1-second delay between relays. The loop repeats indefinitely until the program is interrupted by a signal (e.g., Ctrl+C).

The signal handler function is installed to catch SIGINT and SIGTERM signals, which are sent when the user presses Ctrl+C or the program is terminated externally. When a signal is received, the signal handler deactivates all relays and releases all GPIO lines before exiting the program.

Note that this code assumes that the GPIO chip is /dev/gpiochip0, which is the default on Raspberry Pi 3 Model B+. If your system uses a different GPIO chip, you may need to modify the `chip_name` variable accordingly.

