This code uses the pigpio library to control a 4-channel active-LOW relay module connected to GPIO4, GPIO17, GPIO27, and GPIO22 on a Raspberry Pi 4 Model B. It implements a timed sequence to simulate a simple automated irrigation system, where each relay is activated for 5 minutes (300 seconds) with a 1-minute (60 seconds) pause between each activation, looping back to the 1st relay after the 4th. The relay module is active-LOW (LOW = ON, HIGH = OFF). The code logs every relay state change (activation and deactivation) with timestamps to the system syslog using openlog/syslog with the identifier 'irrigation_system'. The code checks every pigpio call (gpioInitialise, gpioSetMode, gpioWrite, gpioSetTimerFuncEx) and logs failures to syslog before exiting cleanly. The program uses gpioSetTimerFuncEx with a single timer (timer 0) that fires every 60 seconds, maintaining an internal state machine that tracks whether the current action is a relay activation, deactivation, or pause. The program keeps the program alive with a sleep loop checking a volatile sig_atomic_t flag. The code handles SIGINT and SIGTERM: deactivate all relays (set HIGH), release GPIO resources via gpioTerminate(), close syslog, and exit.

