This code uses the `linux/i2c-dev.h` library to access the I2C bus and the `sysfs` interface to control the servo. It also uses the `syslog` library to log messages to the system log. The `signal_handler` function is used to catch `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM` signals and clean up resources before exiting.

Note that you will need to install the `pigpio` library and enable the I2C interface on your Raspberry Pi before running this code. You can do this by running the following commands:

```bash
sudo apt-get install pigpio
sudo pigpiod
```

You will also need to create a log file called `solar_tracker.log` in the current working directory before running the code.

Also, you will need to adjust the `position` variable in the main loop to control the servo position.

Please note that this code is for educational purposes only and may not work as-is on your system. You may need to modify it to suit your specific requirements.

